      "record": {
        "knowledge_point": {
          "db_id": 17592186087841,
          "id": "4e43e393-8752-58bc-b87c-b72f679cbfd9",
          "title": "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"
        },
        "question": {
          ":question/problem": "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{26}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}.",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=26$ and ${b}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=26,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{26}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=\\sqrt{26},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{26}=2\\sqrt{26},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 4=8.$",
          ":question/id": "c9fe109f-d046-532d-a3de-b152dd90f053",
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/id": "a862589f-a0e1-5b16-815e-d8bebe088265",
